※ pl4g-test: compile=ok run=native,qemu exit=64 diag=none

※ An answer that will not fit stops the program, and what it has to fit is the
※ type and not the byte holding it.  Five plus six is eleven, which a byte
※ holds easily and a `u3` does not.
※
※ The operands come from outside the function so that the compiler cannot work
※ the answer out: written down, it would be refused where it was written,
※ which is the other half of the same rule and is what the error test beside
※ this one covers.

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    added(5u3, 6u3)

fn added(a: u3, b: u3) → u6:
    if a + b ≠ 0u3:
        1u6
    else:
        0u6
